{"name":"Guppie Green","hex":"#00ff7f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"guppie","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of guppy.","A foolish, easily surprised, or naive person."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A high-earning gay person living in a major urban area, especially a male."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/guppie"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Guppy","blocks":[{"text":"The guppy, also known as the peacock fish, millionfish or rainbow fish, is a member of the family Poeciliidae and, akin to most New World members of the family, is classified as a livebearer. Male guppies, which are smaller than females, have ornamental caudal and dorsal fins. Wild guppies generally feed on a variety of food sources, including benthic algae and the larvae of aquatic insects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Guppy_pho_0048.jpg/330px-Guppy_pho_0048.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guppy","wikidata":"Q178202"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Guppy_CS_pair_4_20130113.jpg/500px-Guppy_CS_pair_4_20130113.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Guppy_CS_pair_4_20130113.jpg/1280px-Guppy_CS_pair_4_20130113.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Guppy_CS_pair_4_20130113.jpg/500px-Guppy_CS_pair_4_20130113.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sexual dimorphism exhibited in the Trinidadian guppy (above male, below female)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGuppy_CS_pair_4_20130113.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Guppy_breeds.jpg/500px-Guppy_breeds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Guppy_breeds.jpg/500px-Guppy_breeds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Six examples of guppy breeds in the aquarium hobby","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGuppy_breeds.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikip